# An icon alone says what it is

A control or a mark drawn as an icon with no words beside it — a `Ri*` glyph, an inline `<svg>`, a `✓`, a `×`, a `+` — gets two things: an `aria-label` (its name for a screen reader) and a tooltip with that name, shown on hover and on keyboard focus. This is every icon-only thing: a remove `×` on a card, a drag handle, a `+` that opens a menu, a status mark like the green `✓` or the live square.

The tooltip is the site's, not the browser's. `title=` alone is not it: it waits a second, never shows on keyboard focus or touch, and looks like the OS. Where a `Tooltip` goes on, any `title` comes off.

A tooltip goes **above** its control. `Tooltip` does that unasked; pass `at` only for a control in a corner where above is wrong (the `×` pinned at the top right of a dialog takes `at="left"`). When the asked side has no room in the window the tooltip falls to the opposite side on its own, so never pass `at="bottom"` to get one out of the way of the window's edge.

**The one exception: the back caret** (`HomeLink` in `PageFrame.tsx`, the `<` at the left of a page's title). It keeps its `aria-label="home"` and has no tooltip, and no `title` either. The title row is pinned to the top edge of the window, so above the caret there is no room for a tooltip, and under it is the page: nowhere a `home` could sit reads right. Do not put one back.

- On the page: `<Tooltip content="…">` from `shared-components/Tooltip.tsx`. It wraps the control (`display: contents`, so the control's own layout and `absolute` placement stand), draws into the body (or the open `<dialog>`), stays in the window, and hides on Escape.
- Over a video or a picture (dark glass chips): `IconButton`, or `ControlTip` inside a control with `group/control relative`, from `shared-components/VideoControls.tsx`. It reads white on black, where the page's box would not.
- An icon *beside its word* (a menu row, a tab, "log out") needs nothing: the word is the name.
- A `+` that opens a menu on hover puts its name as the first line of the menu instead: a tooltip would fight the menu for the hover.
- Hide the tooltip while it would be in the way: `content={dragging ? null : 'drag to resize'}`, `content={open ? null : 'add a source'}`. Nothing is shown for a null `content`.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — a glyph with no name, or a name only the browser's slow title gives
<button type="button" onClick={remove}><RiCloseLine aria-hidden size={16} /></button>
<button type="button" aria-label="replace" title="replace" onClick={replace}><RiRefreshLine aria-hidden size={16} /></button>

// ✅ GOOD — the name for the reader, the same name on hover and focus
<Tooltip content="remove">
  <button type="button" aria-label="remove" onClick={remove}><RiCloseLine aria-hidden size={16} /></button>
</Tooltip>

// ✅ GOOD — over the picture
<IconButton label="picture in picture" onClick={toCorner}><PipIcon /></IconButton>

// ✅ GOOD — a mark, not a control
<Tooltip content="has an account: this really is them">
  <span role="img" aria-label="has an account" className="text-increase">✓</span>
</Tooltip>
```

The about tab's edit chrome (`About.tsx`: move, replace, remove, resize, the `+`, the four kinds) is the pattern in full: an `Icon` helper that is `Tooltip` around a labelled button.
